/* CSO Cyber Range — Course content: BC-104 Identity & Access Management (IAM) Basics */
window.COURSE_DATA = window.COURSE_DATA || {};
window.COURSE_DATA["BC-104"] = {
ov:"Identity is the real perimeter. In cloud and hybrid environments the network boundary dissolves, and who a subject is and what they may do becomes the primary control plane. This course builds IAM from first principles — the identity lifecycle, authentication factors, access-control models, and modern federation and Zero Trust identity. Each module ends in a required knowledge check; the 40-item capstone draws two items per module plus scenario decisions, so treat the models and mechanisms as testable.",
mods:[
{t:"IAAA: The Identity Lifecycle",d:"Identification, Authentication, Authorization, Accounting.",
obj:["Distinguish the four IAAA phases with correct usage","Explain how the phases build on each other","Map real access failures to the phase that broke"],
read:["Access control decomposes into four sequential phases. Identification is the claim of an identity (entering a username). Authentication is proving that claim with credentials (a password, a key, a biometric). Authorization is deciding what the now-authenticated identity may do (which resources, which actions). Accounting (also called auditing) records what they did. Each depends on the one before: you cannot authorize a subject you have not authenticated, and you cannot meaningfully audit actions you cannot attribute to an identity.",
"Keeping the phases distinct sharpens both design and diagnosis. Many security failures are precisely locatable: a shared account breaks accounting (you cannot attribute actions to a person); weak passwords break authentication (the claim is trivially forged); excessive permissions break authorization (an authenticated user can do more than they should); missing logs break accounting (you cannot reconstruct what happened). When you can name which phase failed, the remedy follows directly rather than as generic tightening.",
"The phases also frame the whole course. Authentication factors and MFA (modules two through four) strengthen the second phase; access-control models and least privilege (modules five, six) govern the third; session management and logging support the fourth. Directory services, federation, and PAM (modules seven through nine) are the machinery that scales all four across an enterprise. Holding the IAAA frame lets you place every later topic and every real-world incident into a coherent structure."],
ex:"An investigation finds that a database was altered by 'the admin account,' shared among five people with a common password. Identification and authentication technically occurred, but accounting failed completely — no one can be attributed the action. The fix is not a stronger password but individual named accounts with individual credentials, restoring the attributability that the shared account destroyed.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-63-3, Digital Identity Guidelines","The authoritative model for identity proofing, authentication, and federation."],["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, AC and IA families","Access control and identification/authentication control requirements."]],
kc:[
{q:"Proving an asserted identity with a credential is which IAAA phase?",o:["Identification","Authentication","Authorization","Accounting"],a:1,x:"Identification is the claim; authentication is proving it with something you know, have, or are."},
{q:"A shared account used by five administrators primarily breaks which phase?",o:["Identification","Authentication","Authorization","Accounting"],a:1,x:"Actions cannot be attributed to an individual, so accounting/auditing fails even if authentication technically succeeded."},
{q:"An authenticated user who can access records far beyond their job indicates a failure of:",o:["Identification","Authentication","Authorization","Accounting"],a:1,x:"The identity was verified, but the decision of what they may do — authorization — granted excessive access."}]},
{t:"Authentication Factors",d:"Something you know, have, and are — and their trade-offs.",
obj:["Categorize authenticators into the three factor types","Explain the strengths and weaknesses of each factor","Explain what makes authentication truly multi-factor"],
read:["Authenticators fall into three categories: something you know (passwords, PINs, security questions), something you have (a hardware token, a phone with an authenticator app, a smart card), and something you are (biometrics — fingerprint, face, iris). A fourth, somewhere you are (location), and behavioral factors increasingly supplement these. Each category has characteristic failure modes: knowledge factors can be guessed, phished, or reused; possession factors can be stolen or, in the case of SMS, intercepted; biometrics cannot be changed once compromised and can produce false matches.",
"True multi-factor authentication requires factors from different categories, not two of the same kind. Two passwords are not MFA — they share the knowledge factor's weaknesses. A password plus a hardware token is MFA, because compromising both requires two independent attacks (phishing the password and physically stealing the token). This is why the categorization matters practically: an attacker who phishes a password has defeated everything protected only by knowledge factors, but must additionally defeat a possession or inherence factor to breach a true-MFA account.",
"Biometrics deserve particular care. They are convenient and hard to forget, but they are identifiers more than secrets — you leave fingerprints everywhere and your face is public — so they are best used as one factor (often the possession-unlock, as on phones) rather than the sole gate to sensitive systems. And because a compromised biometric cannot be reissued like a password, systems store biometric templates, not raw data, and match locally where possible. Understanding factor properties prevents the common error of stacking convenient-but-weak factors and calling it strong."],
ex:"An organization deploys 'two-factor' authentication as a password plus a security question. An attacker who phishes the password harvests the security-question answers from the same fake page — both are knowledge factors defeated by one attack. Replacing the security question with a hardware token or authenticator app makes it genuinely multi-factor, requiring a second, independent compromise.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-63B, Authentication and Lifecycle Management","Authenticator types, assurance levels, and their permitted uses."],["CISA, Multi-Factor Authentication guidance","Practical factor selection and why not all MFA is equal."]],
kc:[
{q:"Which combination is genuinely multi-factor?",o:["A password and a PIN","A password and a security question","A password and a hardware token","Two different passwords"],a:1,x:"Only a password (know) plus a token (have) uses two different categories; passwords, PINs, and security questions are all knowledge factors."},
{q:"A key weakness of biometrics as an authenticator is that:",o:["They are easy to forget","Once compromised they cannot be changed, and they are identifiers rather than secrets","They require a network","They are always slower"],a:1,x:"You cannot reissue a fingerprint, and biometric data is not truly secret — reasons to use it as one factor rather than the sole gate."},
{q:"Two passwords do not constitute MFA because they:",o:["Are too long","Share the knowledge-factor weaknesses, so one phishing attack can capture both","Are encrypted differently","Use different keyboards"],a:1,x:"MFA requires independent categories; two knowledge factors fall to a single attack that captures what the user knows."}]},
{t:"Password Security and Modern Guidance",d:"Length over complexity, breach checks, and storage.",
obj:["State modern NIST password guidance and its rationale","Explain proper password storage (hashing, salting)","Identify password anti-patterns that reduce security"],
read:["Modern password guidance overturned decades of counterproductive rules. NIST SP 800-63B now favors length over arcane complexity: long passphrases are both stronger and more memorable than short strings of forced symbols, which users defeat with predictable substitutions (P@ssw0rd1). It recommends against mandatory periodic rotation absent evidence of compromise, because forced frequent changes drive weak, incremental passwords (Spring2024, Summer2024). It recommends screening new passwords against known-breached lists, since a password already in a breach corpus is instantly guessable regardless of its complexity.",
"How passwords are stored matters more than their composition. Passwords must never be stored in plaintext or reversibly encrypted; they are hashed with a slow, salted, purpose-built algorithm (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2, or PBKDF2). The salt (a unique random value per password) defeats precomputed rainbow tables, and the deliberate slowness defeats mass offline guessing after a database breach. A leaked database of properly-hashed passwords is a manageable incident; a leaked database of plaintext or MD5-hashed passwords is a catastrophe, because attackers crack weak hashes at billions per second.",
"The practical anti-patterns to eliminate: password reuse across sites (one breach compromises many accounts — the reason credential stuffing works), storing passwords in plaintext or with fast hashes, security questions with publicly-discoverable answers, and treating complexity theater as a substitute for MFA. The strategic conclusion is that passwords alone are a weak single factor; the goal is to make them one reasonable factor (long, unique, breach-checked, well-hashed) behind MFA, and ultimately to move toward passwordless, phishing-resistant authentication (module four)."],
ex:"A breached service had stored passwords as unsalted MD5. Attackers cracked 90% within hours using GPU rigs and rainbow tables, then credential-stuffed the reused passwords into victims' email and banking. Had the service used salted Argon2, the same breach would have yielded few cracked passwords and bought users time to reset — the storage decision determined the blast radius.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-63B, Section 5.1.1","Modern password (memorized secret) requirements and the rationale for them."],["OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet","Correct hashing, salting, and algorithm selection for storing credentials."]],
kc:[
{q:"Modern NIST guidance favors which over traditional complexity rules?",o:["Frequent mandatory rotation","Length (passphrases) plus screening against known-breached passwords","Security questions","Shorter passwords for memorability"],a:1,x:"Long passphrases and breach-screening outperform forced symbols and rotation, which users defeat predictably."},
{q:"A salt added before hashing a password primarily defeats:",o:["Network sniffing","Precomputed rainbow-table attacks by making each hash unique","Phishing","Session hijacking"],a:1,x:"A unique per-password salt means identical passwords hash differently, breaking precomputed tables and forcing per-password cracking."},
{q:"Why is mandatory frequent password rotation now discouraged absent evidence of compromise?",o:["It is too much work for admins","It drives users to weak, predictable incremental passwords, reducing real security","Rotation is illegal","It breaks hashing"],a:1,x:"Forced frequent changes produce Spring2024/Summer2024 patterns; rotation without cause degrades rather than improves password strength."}]},
{t:"Multi-Factor and Phishing-Resistant Authentication",d:"Why not all MFA is equal, and what stops modern phishing.",
obj:["Rank common MFA methods by resistance to attack","Explain phishing-resistant authentication (FIDO2/WebAuthn, PKI)","Describe MFA-fatigue and real-time phishing and their defenses"],
read:["MFA dramatically reduces account takeover, but the methods vary widely in strength. SMS one-time codes are the weakest common MFA — vulnerable to SIM-swapping and interception — yet still far better than none. Authenticator-app codes (TOTP) and push notifications are stronger, but both remain phishable: a real-time phishing proxy relays the victim's code to the real site instantly, and push notifications enable MFA-fatigue attacks where the attacker spams approval prompts until a tired user taps accept. The lesson is that most MFA raises the bar without closing the phishing door.",
"Phishing-resistant authentication closes it. FIDO2/WebAuthn (security keys and platform authenticators like Windows Hello) and PKI smart cards bind the authentication cryptographically to the legitimate site's origin, so a credential presented to a phishing domain simply does not work — there is no code for the victim to relay because the challenge is origin-scoped and the private key never leaves the device. This is why security agencies now push phishing-resistant MFA as the standard for privileged and high-value accounts: it defeats the real-time proxy and MFA-fatigue attacks that beat every code-based method.",
"Deploying MFA well means matching method to risk and closing gaps: phishing-resistant factors for administrators and sensitive systems, number-matching and prompt-limiting to blunt MFA-fatigue where push is used, and eliminating fallback paths (a strong-MFA account with an SMS-reset backdoor is only as strong as the backdoor). Conditional access — requiring stronger authentication for risky contexts (new device, unusual location) — ties MFA to the Zero Trust logic of module fourteen. The trajectory of the field is toward passwordless, phishing-resistant authentication as the default, not the exception."],
ex:"An attacker runs a real-time phishing proxy: the victim enters their password and TOTP code on a convincing fake, and the proxy relays both to the real site within seconds, capturing the session. The same attack against a FIDO2 security key fails — the key's response is cryptographically bound to the real origin, so nothing the victim does on the phishing domain produces a usable credential. Method choice, not MFA presence, decided the outcome.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-63B and CISA phishing-resistant MFA guidance","Assurance levels and the push toward FIDO2/WebAuthn and PKI."],["FIDO Alliance, How FIDO Works","Why origin-bound credentials defeat phishing and relay attacks."]],
kc:[
{q:"Which MFA method is phishing-resistant?",o:["SMS one-time codes","Authenticator-app TOTP codes","FIDO2/WebAuthn security keys","Push notifications"],a:1,x:"FIDO2/WebAuthn binds authentication to the legitimate origin cryptographically, so credentials cannot be relayed to a phishing site — code-based methods can."},
{q:"An MFA-fatigue attack succeeds by:",o:["Cracking the password hash","Spamming push-approval prompts until a tired or confused user taps accept","Intercepting SMS","Stealing a security key"],a:1,x:"Repeated push prompts exploit user annoyance; number-matching and prompt-limiting defenses counter it, but phishing-resistant methods avoid it entirely."},
{q:"A strong-MFA account with an SMS-based password-reset fallback is:",o:["Fully secure","Only as strong as the weakest recovery path — the SMS backdoor can bypass the strong MFA","Impossible to configure","Compliant by default"],a:1,x:"Attackers target the weakest path; a strong front door with a weak reset backdoor is defeated through the backdoor."}]},
{t:"Access Control Models: DAC, MAC, RBAC, ABAC",d:"How authorization decisions are structured.",
obj:["Distinguish DAC, MAC, RBAC, and ABAC","Match each model to an appropriate use case","Explain the scalability trade-offs of each"],
read:["Authorization is structured by a model. Discretionary Access Control (DAC) lets resource owners grant access at their discretion — the familiar file-permission model, flexible but prone to sprawl as owners share ad hoc. Mandatory Access Control (MAC) enforces access by system-wide policy based on labels (classification levels), where users cannot override the rules — used in high-security and military contexts where a Secret user cannot read Top Secret regardless of any owner's wishes. These sit at opposite ends of the flexibility-versus-control spectrum.",
"Role-Based Access Control (RBAC) assigns permissions to roles and users to roles, so access follows job function rather than individual discretion. RBAC scales far better than DAC in organizations because you manage a manageable number of roles, not thousands of individual grants, and it maps naturally to least privilege — a role carries exactly the permissions its function needs. Attribute-Based Access Control (ABAC) generalizes further: decisions are computed from attributes of the user, resource, action, and environment (a contractor, in this department, during business hours, from a managed device), enabling fine-grained, context-aware policy.",
"Real systems blend these. RBAC is the enterprise default because it balances manageability and precision; ABAC layers on for context-sensitive decisions and is the engine behind Zero Trust conditional access; DAC persists for user-owned resources; MAC appears where classification demands it. The design skill is choosing the model that matches the environment's scale and sensitivity — DAC's flexibility becomes ungovernable sprawl at enterprise scale, while MAC's rigidity is overkill for a small team. Getting the model right is prerequisite to enforcing least privilege, the subject of the next module."],
ex:"A hospital uses RBAC: nurse, physician, billing-clerk, and administrator roles each carry a defined permission set, and staff are assigned roles by position. When a nurse is promoted, changing their role updates their access in one action. An ABAC layer adds context — physicians can access records only for patients in their assigned unit during their shift — combining role-based structure with attribute-based precision.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-162, Guide to ABAC","Attribute-based access control definitions and architecture."],["NIST RBAC model (INCITS 359)","The formal role-based access control model and its scalability properties."]],
kc:[
{q:"RBAC assigns permissions based on:",o:["The resource owner's discretion","A user's assigned role within the organization","Security classification labels only","Geographic location alone"],a:1,x:"RBAC ties permissions to roles and users to roles, so access follows job function — scalable and aligned with least privilege."},
{q:"Mandatory Access Control (MAC) differs from DAC in that MAC:",o:["Lets owners grant access freely","Enforces access by system-wide policy/labels that users cannot override","Is only used in small teams","Requires no authentication"],a:1,x:"MAC enforces classification-based rules centrally; unlike DAC, individual owners cannot grant access against policy."},
{q:"ABAC makes access decisions primarily based on:",o:["Static roles only","Attributes of user, resource, action, and environment evaluated together","The owner's mood","Password length"],a:1,x:"ABAC computes decisions from combined attributes, enabling fine-grained, context-aware authorization — the basis of Zero Trust conditional access."}]},
{t:"Least Privilege and Separation of Duties",d:"Minimizing standing access and splitting dangerous power.",
obj:["Define least privilege and apply it to accounts and roles","Define separation of duties and identify where it is required","Explain privilege creep and how to detect it"],
read:["Least privilege — a subject should hold only the access required to perform its function — is the single most important authorization principle, because it caps what any compromised account can do. Applied rigorously it means users are not local admins, service accounts have narrowly-scoped permissions, and even administrators use elevated privilege only when needed (just-in-time) rather than holding it standing. Every earlier course reinforced this: the over-privileged Backup Operators account, the broad sudo grant, the domain admin on a workstation — all were least-privilege failures.",
"Separation of duties (SoD) splits a sensitive process across multiple people so no single individual can complete it alone, preventing both fraud and single-point error. The classic examples are financial (the person who creates a vendor cannot also approve payments to it) and security (the person who requests access cannot also approve it; the developer who writes code cannot also deploy it to production unreviewed). SoD is enforced through role design and workflow, and it is a required control in regulated environments precisely because it defends against insider threat and mistake simultaneously.",
"The enemy of both principles is privilege creep: over time, as people change roles and accumulate access that is never revoked, their permissions grow far beyond their current function. A ten-year employee often holds access from every past role — a lucrative target. Detecting and reversing creep requires periodic access reviews (recertification), where managers or resource owners confirm that each grant is still justified, and automated flagging of accounts with excessive or unused permissions. The joiner-mover-leaver discipline of module ten exists largely to prevent creep at its source."],
ex:"An access review finds that a finance manager, over three role changes, accumulated the ability to both create vendors and approve payments to them — a separation-of-duties violation that enables undetectable fraud. The review revokes the conflicting grant and the workflow is redesigned so the two actions require different people. The creep was invisible until the recertification forced someone to look.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, AC-5, AC-6","Separation of duties and least privilege control requirements."],["ISACA, Segregation of Duties guidance","How SoD is designed and audited in enterprise and financial systems."]],
kc:[
{q:"The principle of least privilege states that a subject should have:",o:["Maximum access to reduce help-desk tickets","Only the access required to perform its function","Administrative rights by default","No access to anything at all"],a:1,x:"Least privilege caps the blast radius of any compromise by granting only what the function requires — nothing standing beyond that."},
{q:"Separation of duties prevents fraud and error by:",o:["Encrypting all transactions","Splitting a sensitive process so no single person can complete it alone","Requiring longer passwords","Logging every action"],a:1,x:"Dividing critical actions (e.g., create vendor vs. approve payment) across people blocks unilateral fraud and single-point mistakes."},
{q:"Privilege creep is best detected and reversed by:",o:["Longer passwords","Periodic access reviews (recertification) confirming each grant is still justified","Disabling MFA","Adding more roles"],a:1,x:"Recertification forces owners to re-justify accumulated access, catching the excess permissions that pile up as people change roles."}]},
{t:"Directory Services and Active Directory",d:"LDAP, domains, and the identity backbone.",
obj:["Explain the role of directory services in centralized identity","Describe Active Directory structure and Kerberos authentication at a high level","Explain why the directory is the enterprise's most critical asset"],
read:["Directory services centralize identity: rather than each system maintaining its own accounts, a directory (Active Directory in most enterprises, LDAP more broadly) is the authoritative store of users, groups, computers, and their attributes, and systems authenticate and authorize against it. This centralization is enormously efficient — one account, one password policy, one place to disable a departing employee — and it is why the directory becomes the beating heart of enterprise identity, and therefore the ultimate target.",
"Active Directory organizes objects into domains and organizational units, applies configuration through Group Policy (BC-103), and authenticates primarily via Kerberos, a ticket-based protocol where a central authority issues time-limited tickets that prove identity to services without resending passwords. Understanding Kerberos at a high level matters because its tickets are exactly what credential-theft attacks target (pass-the-ticket, Kerberoasting), and because a compromise of the domain's key material (the KRBTGT account) lets an attacker forge tickets for any identity — the golden-ticket attack that means total, persistent domain control.",
"Because the directory authenticates everything, compromising it compromises everything — which is why domain controllers are guarded as crown jewels, domain-admin credentials are handled with extreme care (module thirteen), and directory attacks (privilege escalation to Domain Admin, DCSync to extract all credentials) are the endgame of most enterprise intrusions. Defenders harden the directory (tiered administration, privileged access workstations, monitoring of directory changes and replication) and treat any anomaly in domain-admin activity as a potential catastrophe. The directory is where the earlier courses' credential, privilege, and logging lessons converge."],
ex:"An attacker escalates to Domain Admin, then runs DCSync to replicate the entire directory's password hashes — including KRBTGT. With KRBTGT they forge golden tickets granting themselves any identity indefinitely, surviving password resets. This is why directory compromise is treated as total compromise: control of the identity backbone means control of every system that trusts it.",
refs:[["Microsoft, Active Directory security best practices","Tiered administration, privileged access, and directory hardening."],["MITRE ATT&CK, Credential Access and Kerberos techniques","Kerberoasting, pass-the-ticket, DCSync, and golden-ticket attacks."]],
kc:[
{q:"The primary benefit of a centralized directory service is:",o:["Faster networking","One authoritative identity store, so accounts and policy are managed once and disabling a user takes effect everywhere","Encrypted disks","Automatic patching"],a:1,x:"Centralization means single-point management of identity — one account, one policy, one place to revoke access."},
{q:"Compromise of the KRBTGT account is catastrophic because it lets an attacker:",o:["Read one user's email","Forge Kerberos tickets for any identity (golden tickets), achieving persistent total domain control","Change the network cabling","Disable a single workstation"],a:1,x:"KRBTGT holds the key that signs Kerberos tickets; possessing it means forging authentication for anyone, surviving password resets."},
{q:"Why are domain controllers treated as crown-jewel assets?",o:["They are expensive hardware","Because the directory authenticates everything, so compromising it compromises the entire environment","They store user documents","They run the website"],a:1,x:"The directory is the trust root for the enterprise; its compromise cascades to every system that relies on it for authentication."}]},
{t:"Single Sign-On and Federation",d:"SAML, OIDC, and trust across boundaries.",
obj:["Explain SSO and the user and security benefits","Describe SAML and OpenID Connect federation at a high level","Identify the concentrated risk SSO creates"],
read:["Single Sign-On lets a user authenticate once to an identity provider and then access many applications without re-authenticating to each. The benefits are real: fewer passwords to manage and reuse, centralized policy and MFA enforcement, and one place to disable access. Federation extends SSO across organizational boundaries — an identity provider in one organization vouches for a user to a service provider in another, enabling secure access to partner and cloud services without provisioning separate accounts everywhere.",
"Two protocols dominate. SAML (Security Assertion Markup Language) is the established enterprise standard, exchanging signed XML assertions between identity and service providers. OpenID Connect (OIDC), built on OAuth 2.0, is the modern token-based approach common in web and mobile, issuing signed JSON tokens. Both rest on cryptographic trust: the service provider trusts assertions/tokens signed by the identity provider it has federated with. Understanding this trust relationship matters because attacks target it — forged or replayed tokens, misconfigured trust, and identity-provider compromise all subvert the whole federation.",
"SSO concentrates both convenience and risk. Because one authentication unlocks everything, the identity provider becomes an extraordinarily high-value target: compromise the IdP or a user's IdP session, and the attacker inherits access to every federated application. This is why SSO must be paired with strong, phishing-resistant MFA at the identity provider, tight session management (module twelve), and monitoring of the IdP for anomalous authentication. SSO does not eliminate risk; it relocates it to the identity provider, which must then be defended commensurately — the same crown-jewel logic as the directory."],
ex:"An attacker phishes a user's identity-provider credentials and session. Because the organization uses SSO across 40 SaaS applications, that single session grants access to email, file storage, HR, and finance systems simultaneously — the concentration SSO creates. Phishing-resistant MFA at the IdP would have blocked the credential phish, and short IdP session lifetimes with re-authentication for sensitive apps would have limited the damage.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-63C, Federation and Assertions","Federation models, assertion security, and assurance levels."],["OAuth 2.0 / OpenID Connect specifications and OWASP guidance","How token-based federation works and its common misconfigurations."]],
kc:[
{q:"Single Sign-On improves security primarily by:",o:["Eliminating the need for authentication","Reducing password sprawl/reuse and centralizing MFA and access-revocation at one identity provider","Encrypting the network","Removing the need for logging"],a:1,x:"One strong authentication point replaces many weak ones, centralizing policy, MFA, and the ability to disable access."},
{q:"SAML and OpenID Connect both fundamentally rely on:",o:["Shared passwords between apps","Cryptographic trust — the service provider trusts assertions/tokens signed by the federated identity provider","Physical tokens only","Disabling encryption"],a:1,x:"Federation is built on signed assertions/tokens; the service provider trusts what the IdP cryptographically vouches for."},
{q:"The concentrated risk of SSO is that:",o:["It is slower","Compromise of the identity provider or a user's IdP session grants access to every federated application at once","It requires no MFA","It cannot be logged"],a:1,x:"Because one authentication unlocks everything, the IdP becomes a crown-jewel target requiring phishing-resistant MFA and tight session control."}]},
{t:"Privileged Access Management",d:"Vaulting, just-in-time elevation, and session control for admins.",
obj:["Explain what PAM systems provide beyond normal IAM","Describe credential vaulting and just-in-time privileged access","Explain privileged session monitoring and its value"],
read:["Privileged accounts — administrators, root, service accounts with broad rights — are the accounts attackers want most, and Privileged Access Management exists to control them specifically. A PAM system vaults privileged credentials so they are not known to or stored by humans: an administrator checks out access when needed, the PAM injects the credential without revealing it, and rotates it after use. This means a phished admin cannot surrender a password they never knew, and stolen cached credentials become useless after rotation.",
"PAM enforces just-in-time, least-privilege elevation: rather than administrators holding standing privileged access (the risk emphasized throughout this course), they request elevation for a specific task and duration, it is granted through an approval workflow, and it expires automatically. This shrinks the window in which powerful credentials exist to be stolen and creates an approval and audit trail for every privileged action. Combined with tiered administration and privileged access workstations (BC-103), it operationalizes the principle that powerful access should be rare, brief, approved, and logged.",
"Privileged session management adds monitoring and control: privileged sessions are brokered through the PAM, recorded (keystrokes, screen), and can be terminated in real time if anomalous. This provides both deterrence and forensic evidence — an administrator (or an attacker using stolen admin access) knows the session is watched, and investigators have a complete record. For high-value systems this transforms privileged access from an unmonitored trust into a controlled, accountable, recorded operation, which is exactly what regulators and incident responders require of the accounts that can do the most damage."],
ex:"An attacker compromises an admin's workstation but finds no usable privileged credentials — they are vaulted in the PAM and rotated after each use. To act, the attacker would need to pass the PAM's approval workflow and MFA, and any session would be recorded and terminable. The PAM converted a workstation compromise from an automatic domain takeover into a contained, monitored dead end.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, AC-2/AC-6 and privileged access guidance","Requirements for managing and monitoring privileged accounts."],["Gartner/vendor PAM architecture references","Vaulting, just-in-time elevation, and session monitoring patterns."]],
kc:[
{q:"Credential vaulting in a PAM system protects against phished admins because:",o:["Admins type faster","The admin never knows the credential the PAM injects, and it is rotated after use","It disables the network","It encrypts the disk"],a:1,x:"You cannot phish a password the human never knew, and post-use rotation makes any captured credential quickly worthless."},
{q:"Just-in-time privileged access reduces risk by:",o:["Giving admins permanent rights for convenience","Granting elevation only for a specific task and duration, so powerful credentials rarely exist to be stolen","Removing all logging","Sharing admin accounts"],a:1,x:"Time-boxed, approved elevation shrinks the window of exposure and creates an audit trail, replacing risky standing privilege."},
{q:"Privileged session recording provides:",o:["Faster sessions","Deterrence plus complete forensic evidence, and the ability to terminate anomalous sessions in real time","Encryption of email","Automatic patching"],a:1,x:"Recorded, terminable privileged sessions deter misuse and give investigators a full record of what powerful accounts actually did."}]},
{t:"Identity Lifecycle: Joiner, Mover, Leaver",d:"Provisioning, changes, and timely deprovisioning.",
obj:["Describe the joiner-mover-leaver process and its controls","Explain why timely deprovisioning is a critical security control","Explain automated provisioning and access recertification"],
read:["Identities have a lifecycle, and each stage is a control point. Joiner: a new person is provisioned with access appropriate to their role — ideally via automated, role-based provisioning that grants a standard baseline rather than ad hoc copying of a colleague's access (which propagates privilege creep). Mover: when someone changes roles, their access must be adjusted — critically, old access removed, not just new access added, or they accumulate the excessive permissions this course keeps warning about. Leaver: when someone departs, their access must be revoked promptly and completely.",
"Timely deprovisioning is one of the most commonly failed and most consequential controls. Orphaned accounts of departed employees and contractors are a favored attack vector: they are unmonitored, their passwords may be known to the former holder, and they retain whatever access they had. A departing administrator whose access lingers, or a contractor account never disabled, is a standing backdoor. The control is a tight, automated linkage between the authoritative HR/contract system and the directory, so that a termination event triggers immediate, comprehensive access revocation — including federated apps, VPN, and email.",
"Because provisioning and changes inevitably produce drift and error, access recertification (module six's antidote to creep) closes the loop: periodically, managers and resource owners review who has access to what and attest that each grant is still warranted, revoking what is not. Automated identity governance tools drive this at scale, flagging orphaned accounts, dormant accounts, and separation-of-duties conflicts. The lifecycle discipline is unglamorous but decisive — most excessive-access and orphaned-account findings trace directly to a lifecycle process that provisioned generously and deprovisioned slowly."],
ex:"A contractor's engagement ends, but because provisioning was manual and disconnected from the contract system, the account is never disabled. Six months later it is used — from a foreign IP — as the entry point for an intrusion, still holding its original access. An automated leaver process tied to the contract end date would have revoked it the day the engagement closed, eliminating the standing backdoor.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-53 Rev.5, AC-2 (Account Management)","Account provisioning, modification, disabling, and review requirements."],["Identity governance (IGA) reference practices","Automated joiner-mover-leaver and access recertification at scale."]],
kc:[
{q:"When an employee changes roles (mover), the critical often-missed step is:",o:["Adding new access","Removing the access from their previous role, not merely adding new permissions","Resetting their password","Changing their desk"],a:1,x:"Adding without removing produces privilege creep; the mover process must strip obsolete access to maintain least privilege."},
{q:"Orphaned accounts of departed staff are dangerous because they are:",o:["Encrypted","Unmonitored, retain their access, and may have credentials known to the former holder — a standing backdoor","Automatically safe","Required for audits"],a:1,x:"Un-disabled accounts of leavers are unwatched entry points with intact access, a favored attack vector — hence prompt deprovisioning."},
{q:"Access recertification exists to:",o:["Reset all passwords","Periodically confirm each access grant is still justified, catching drift, creep, and orphaned accounts","Encrypt the directory","Speed up login"],a:1,x:"Recertification forces owners to re-justify access, reversing the accumulation and orphaning that provisioning processes produce over time."}]},
{t:"Non-Human and Service Accounts",d:"Machine identities, secrets, and their outsized risk.",
obj:["Explain why non-human identities outnumber and outrisk human ones","Describe secure secret management for service accounts","Identify service-account anti-patterns"],
read:["Non-human identities — service accounts, application accounts, API keys, certificates, cloud workload identities — now vastly outnumber human users in most environments, and they are often the weakest link. They tend to have broad, standing privileges (a service account that runs a critical application may have extensive access), rarely have their credentials rotated, are frequently exempt from MFA (they cannot tap a phone), and their secrets are often embedded in code, scripts, and configuration files where they leak. Attackers specifically hunt these because they combine high privilege with weak protection.",
"Managing machine identities properly means treating their secrets with rigor. Secrets belong in a secrets manager or vault (not in source code or config files), are injected at runtime, and are rotated automatically. Modern approaches eliminate long-lived secrets entirely: cloud workload identity federation and short-lived, automatically-issued credentials mean an application authenticates by its verified identity rather than a static key that can be stolen and reused. Certificate-based authentication with automated lifecycle management (renewal, revocation) similarly avoids the leaked-static-secret problem.",
"The anti-patterns are pervasive and dangerous: hardcoded credentials in repositories (a leading breach cause, since public and even private repos leak), service accounts with domain-admin rights held standing, shared service accounts used across many systems, and secrets that have never been rotated in years. Each is a least-privilege or secret-hygiene failure applied to the identities that are hardest to monitor. As environments become more automated and cloud-native (the AC tier), non-human identity governance becomes as important as human IAM — arguably more so, given the sheer numbers and the standing privilege involved."],
ex:"A developer commits a service account's API key to a repository. Automated scanners that adversaries run against code hosts find it within minutes, and because the key had broad, unrotated, standing privileges, the attacker uses it directly to access cloud storage. A secrets manager with short-lived injected credentials would have meant there was no static key to leak, and least-privilege scoping would have limited what a leaked key could do.",
refs:[["OWASP Secrets Management Cheat Sheet","Storing, injecting, and rotating machine secrets securely."],["Cloud provider workload identity federation docs","Eliminating long-lived secrets with short-lived, identity-based credentials."]],
kc:[
{q:"Non-human (service) identities are often the weakest link because they:",o:["Are always disabled","Tend to have broad standing privileges, are exempt from MFA, and have secrets embedded where they leak","Cannot authenticate","Are monitored more closely than users"],a:1,x:"High privilege plus weak protection (no MFA, hardcoded, un-rotated secrets) makes service accounts a prime, under-watched target."},
{q:"The most robust modern approach to service-account secrets is:",o:["Hardcoding them in config for reliability","Eliminating long-lived secrets via short-lived, automatically-issued, identity-based credentials","Emailing them to the team","Using the same key everywhere"],a:1,x:"Short-lived credentials issued to a verified workload identity remove the static secret that can be stolen and reused."},
{q:"Hardcoded credentials in a code repository are dangerous because:",o:["They slow the build","Adversaries scan code hosts and find leaked keys within minutes, and such keys often have broad standing access","They cannot be used remotely","Repositories are always private"],a:1,x:"Leaked repository secrets are found and abused rapidly; a secrets manager plus least privilege removes both the leak and its impact."}]},
{t:"Session and Token Management",d:"Cookies, tokens, timeouts, and hijacking defenses.",
obj:["Explain how sessions and tokens maintain authenticated state","Describe session-hijacking and token-theft risks","Identify session-management best practices"],
read:["Authentication happens once, but authenticated state must persist across many requests — and that state is a session, tracked by a session cookie or token. This makes the token itself a credential: whoever holds a valid session token is the user, without needing the password or MFA again. Session hijacking (stealing the token via cross-site scripting, network interception, or malware) and token theft therefore bypass authentication entirely, which is why so much web-attack effort targets sessions rather than passwords — and why the SSO session discussed in module eight is such a high-value target.",
"Protecting sessions requires several measures working together: tokens transmitted only over TLS and marked HttpOnly (inaccessible to JavaScript, defeating XSS theft) and Secure and SameSite (limiting cross-site abuse); tokens that are long, random, and unpredictable; and server-side session invalidation so that logout and administrative revocation actually kill the session. Bearer tokens in modern APIs (OAuth access tokens, JWTs) carry the same risk — a stolen bearer token is usable until it expires, which is why short lifetimes and refresh-token rotation matter.",
"Lifetime and context management balance security against usability. Short session timeouts and re-authentication for sensitive actions (step-up authentication) limit the value of a stolen token; binding sessions to context (device, IP, behavior) and invalidating on anomaly ties session management to Zero Trust. Absolute and idle timeouts, re-authentication for privilege changes, and immediate invalidation on password reset or detected compromise are the practical controls. The overarching lesson is that authentication is not a one-time gate but a continuously-maintained state, and the session is a credential that must be protected as carefully as the password that created it."],
ex:"A cross-site scripting flaw lets an attacker steal a user's session cookie and impersonate them without any password — until the team marks cookies HttpOnly (blocking the JavaScript theft) and shortens session lifetimes with server-side invalidation. The stolen-token attack, which defeats even MFA, was closed by treating the session token as the sensitive credential it is.",
refs:[["OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet","Secure session token generation, attributes, lifetime, and invalidation."],["OWASP JWT / OAuth token security guidance","Protecting bearer tokens, expiry, and refresh-token rotation."]],
kc:[
{q:"A session token is effectively a credential because:",o:["It contains the password","Whoever holds a valid token is treated as the authenticated user without re-entering password or MFA","It is always encrypted","It expires instantly"],a:1,x:"Session state is proof of prior authentication; a stolen valid token grants access directly, bypassing the login and MFA."},
{q:"Marking a session cookie HttpOnly primarily defends against:",o:["Network eavesdropping","Theft via cross-site scripting, by making the cookie inaccessible to JavaScript","Weak passwords","Phishing"],a:1,x:"HttpOnly prevents client-side scripts from reading the cookie, closing the XSS-based session-theft path (TLS handles eavesdropping)."},
{q:"Why does step-up (re)authentication for sensitive actions improve security?",o:["It speeds up the app","A stolen session alone cannot perform high-risk actions without a fresh, stronger authentication","It removes the need for tokens","It disables logging"],a:1,x:"Requiring re-authentication for sensitive operations limits what a hijacked session can do, adding a barrier where it matters most."}]},
{t:"Zero Trust and Continuous Authentication",d:"Never trust, always verify — identity as the control plane.",
obj:["State the core tenets of Zero Trust as they apply to identity","Explain continuous, context-aware authorization","Contrast Zero Trust with perimeter-based trust"],
read:["Zero Trust rejects the perimeter model's assumption that being inside the network implies trust. Its core tenet — never trust, always verify — makes every access request subject to authentication and authorization regardless of network location, evaluated against identity, device health, and context. In a Zero Trust model there is no trusted inside; a request from the corporate LAN is scrutinized as thoroughly as one from the internet, because the network location tells you nothing about whether the request is legitimate.",
"For identity this means authorization becomes continuous and context-aware rather than a one-time gate. Conditional access policies (the ABAC engine of module five) evaluate each request against signals — who the user is, whether their device is managed and healthy, where and when they are connecting, and risk indicators — and grant, deny, or step up authentication accordingly. Access is also least-privilege and just-in-time (modules six, nine): a subject gets exactly the access needed for exactly as long as needed, re-verified continuously, so a session that becomes anomalous can be challenged or cut mid-stream.",
"Zero Trust reframes the entire security architecture around identity as the primary control plane, which is why this course sits where it does. The network defenses of BC-102, the endpoint hardening of BC-103, and the identity controls here all feed the Zero Trust decision: the policy engine consults device posture (NAC/EDR), identity and MFA strength, and behavioral context before every consequential access. It is not a product but an architecture and a mindset — assume breach, verify explicitly, enforce least privilege — and it is the direction the entire field, and the rest of this academy, is moving."],
ex:"A user's valid credentials are used to access finance data, but the conditional-access engine notes the request comes from an unmanaged device in an unusual country at 3 a.m. — a risk profile inconsistent with the user's baseline. Zero Trust logic denies the access and forces phishing-resistant step-up authentication, blocking an attacker using stolen credentials even though the password and session were valid. Network location granted no free pass.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-207, Zero Trust Architecture","The authoritative tenets and logical components of Zero Trust."],["CISA Zero Trust Maturity Model","How identity, device, network, and data pillars mature toward Zero Trust."]],
kc:[
{q:"The core tenet of Zero Trust is:",o:["Trust everything inside the corporate network","Never trust, always verify — every request is authenticated and authorized regardless of network location","Trust any device with a valid IP","Authenticate once and trust indefinitely"],a:1,x:"Zero Trust removes location-based implicit trust; each request is verified against identity, device, and context every time."},
{q:"Continuous, context-aware authorization means access decisions consider:",o:["Only the password","Identity, device health, location, time, and risk signals evaluated on each request, with step-up or denial as warranted","The user's job title alone","Nothing after initial login"],a:1,x:"Conditional access evaluates combined signals per request, enabling dynamic responses like step-up authentication for risky contexts."},
{q:"How does Zero Trust treat a request originating from the internal LAN?",o:["It is automatically trusted","It is scrutinized as thoroughly as an external request, since location implies no trust","It bypasses MFA","It is blocked by default"],a:1,x:"Zero Trust assumes breach and grants no free pass to internal traffic; the LAN request is verified like any other."}]},
{t:"Identity Threat Detection and Monitoring",d:"Catching identity attacks in the logs.",
obj:["Identify the key identity events worth monitoring","Describe common identity-attack signatures in authentication logs","Explain risk-based detection and automated response for identity"],
read:["Identity is now the primary attack surface, so identity telemetry is primary detection data. The events worth monitoring flow from every prior module: failed and successful authentications (with method and location), MFA challenges and denials, privileged-group changes, new service principals and credentials, conditional-access blocks, impossible-travel logins (the same account authenticating from distant locations faster than travel allows), and directory changes to high-value objects. Aggregating identity-provider, directory, and endpoint authentication logs into a SIEM turns identity into a monitored control plane rather than a silent one.",
"Identity attacks have recognizable signatures. Password spraying appears as many accounts each seeing a few failed logins from one source (evading per-account lockout). Credential stuffing appears as high-volume login attempts with breached credential pairs. MFA-fatigue shows as bursts of push prompts to one user. Kerberoasting and abnormal ticket requests, sudden privileged-group additions, mass access to resources (data-staging), and logins from anonymizing infrastructure are all detectable in the logs — if you are collecting and baselining them, echoing the baseline discipline from network defense.",
"Modern identity protection adds risk scoring and automated response: platforms compute a risk level per sign-in and per user from combined signals and can automatically require step-up authentication, block, or disable a compromised account in real time — the enforcement arm of Zero Trust. This closes the loop from detection to response at machine speed, which matters because identity attacks move fast (a phished session is abused within minutes). The takeaway is that IAM is not only prevention (authentication, authorization) but detection and response on identity events, feeding directly into the SOC and hunting practices of the operational tier."],
ex:"A SIEM flags that a single source is attempting one login each against 400 accounts over an hour — password spraying designed to stay under per-account lockout thresholds. Because identity logs were aggregated and baselined, the pattern is obvious in aggregate though invisible per account, and automated risk policy forces step-up authentication and blocks the source. Collecting and correlating identity events turned a stealthy attack into an easy catch.",
refs:[["MITRE ATT&CK, Credential Access & Initial Access (identity techniques)","Password spraying, credential stuffing, and MFA-abuse signatures."],["Microsoft Entra ID Protection / identity threat detection docs","Risk-based sign-in and user risk scoring with automated response."]],
kc:[
{q:"Password spraying evades per-account lockout by:",o:["Guessing many passwords against one account","Trying a few common passwords against many accounts, keeping per-account failures low","Using stolen session tokens","Disabling MFA"],a:1,x:"Spreading a few attempts across many accounts stays under lockout thresholds; the attack is visible only when logs are aggregated."},
{q:"An impossible-travel alert fires when:",o:["A user forgets their password","One account authenticates from geographically distant locations faster than travel between them allows","MFA is enabled","A session expires"],a:1,x:"Two logins too far apart in space for the time elapsed indicate credential compromise or session theft — a classic identity anomaly."},
{q:"Risk-based identity protection improves response by:",o:["Logging more slowly","Scoring each sign-in from combined signals and automatically stepping up, blocking, or disabling in real time","Removing MFA","Sharing credentials"],a:1,x:"Automated risk scoring enables machine-speed response to fast-moving identity attacks, the enforcement arm of Zero Trust."}]},
{t:"IAM Architecture Capstone",d:"Designing an enterprise identity program end to end.",
obj:["Synthesize the course into a coherent IAM architecture","Sequence identity controls by dependency and value","Verify an IAM design against least-privilege and Zero Trust principles"],
read:["This module assembles the course into an architecture. Start with an authoritative identity source and centralized directory (module seven) fed by an automated joiner-mover-leaver lifecycle tied to HR (module ten), so identities are accurate and promptly deprovisioned. Layer strong, phishing-resistant authentication and SSO at the identity provider (modules two through four, eight), enforce authorization through RBAC with ABAC conditional access (modules five, fourteen), and hold to least privilege and separation of duties everywhere (module six). Wrap privileged and non-human identities in PAM and secrets management (modules nine, eleven), protect sessions (module twelve), and defend credentials at the endpoint (module thirteen), all under continuous monitoring.",
"The dependencies drive the sequence. Least privilege is meaningless without accurate identities and a working lifecycle, so the authoritative source and joiner-mover-leaver come first. SSO concentrates risk at the IdP, so phishing-resistant MFA there is prerequisite to expanding federation. Access recertification presupposes that you know who has what, which presupposes centralization. Building these out of order — federating widely before securing the IdP, or enforcing RBAC before cleaning up orphaned accounts — produces a program that is complex but not secure. Coherence, not feature count, is the goal.",
"Verification tests the design against principles rather than checklists: can any single compromised account or credential reach far beyond its function (least privilege)? Can any one person complete a sensitive process alone (separation of duties)? Does every access decision consider identity, device, and context (Zero Trust)? Are privileged and machine identities vaulted, scoped, and rotated? Is deprovisioning provably prompt? The graded deliverable — an enterprise RBAC and conditional-access design with a lifecycle and privileged-access plan — is exactly this architecture, and its quality is judged by how well its independent controls contain the failure of any one of them."],
ex:"An IAM design review traces a hypothetical phished-credential attack through the architecture: phishing-resistant MFA at the IdP blocks the initial phish; even if bypassed, conditional access flags the anomalous context; RBAC limits the account's reach; PAM keeps privileged credentials out of the attacker's hands; and prompt deprovisioning ensures no orphaned accounts offer an easier path. The design is judged sound because no single control's failure yields catastrophe — the IAM expression of defense in depth.",
refs:[["NIST SP 800-63 series and SP 800-207","Digital identity assurance and Zero Trust architecture as the design backbone."],["Microsoft/cloud IAM reference architectures","Concrete enterprise identity, SSO, PAM, and conditional-access patterns."]],
kc:[
{q:"Why must an authoritative identity source and joiner-mover-leaver lifecycle come first in an IAM architecture?",o:["They are the cheapest","Least privilege, recertification, and access decisions all depend on accurate identities and prompt deprovisioning","They require no security","Federation cannot work without them alone"],a:1,x:"Every downstream control assumes identities are correct and stale ones are removed; without that foundation, the rest is built on sand."},
{q:"Before expanding federation broadly, the prerequisite control is:",o:["Disabling MFA for convenience","Phishing-resistant MFA at the identity provider, since SSO concentrates risk there","Sharing service accounts","Removing recertification"],a:1,x:"SSO makes the IdP a crown jewel; strong phishing-resistant authentication there must precede widening the access it unlocks."},
{q:"A sound IAM design is judged primarily by whether:",o:["It uses the most products","No single compromised account, credential, or control failure yields catastrophe — independent controls contain each other","It has the longest passwords","It avoids logging"],a:1,x:"Defense in depth applied to identity means the failure of any one control (MFA, RBAC, PAM, deprovisioning) is contained by the others."}]}
],
cap:[
{q:"SCENARIO: A database change is attributed only to a shared 'admin' account used by five people. The IAAA phase that failed and the fix:",o:["Authentication; stronger password","Accounting; replace the shared account with individually named accounts and credentials","Authorization; add more roles","Identification; add a CAPTCHA"],a:1,x:"Shared accounts destroy attributability; individual named accounts restore the accounting phase so actions map to people."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker runs a real-time phishing proxy and captures a user's password and TOTP code, hijacking the session. The control that would have stopped it:",o:["A longer password","FIDO2/WebAuthn phishing-resistant authentication bound to the real origin","SMS codes instead of TOTP","Security questions"],a:1,x:"Origin-bound credentials produce nothing a proxy can relay; code-based MFA (SMS/TOTP/push) is defeated by real-time relay."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A ten-year employee, after three role changes, can both create vendors and approve payments. This is primarily a failure of:",o:["Authentication strength","Separation of duties, compounded by privilege creep from movers that added access without removing it","Encryption","Network segmentation"],a:1,x:"One person completing a sensitive process alone violates SoD; the accumulation came from mover processes that never stripped old access."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker escalates to Domain Admin and runs DCSync, then forges golden tickets. The reason this equals total compromise:",o:["It deletes files","Control of the directory's key material lets them forge authentication for any identity, persistently","It only affects one server","It changes the network cabling"],a:1,x:"The directory is the trust root; KRBTGT/golden-ticket forging grants any identity indefinitely, surviving password resets."},
{q:"SCENARIO: One phished IdP session grants an attacker access to 40 SaaS apps at once. The lesson about SSO is:",o:["SSO is always a mistake","SSO relocates risk to the identity provider, which must have phishing-resistant MFA and tight session control","Federation cannot be secured","More apps make it safer"],a:1,x:"SSO concentrates access at the IdP; the concentration is manageable only if the IdP is defended with strong MFA and short sessions."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An attacker compromises an admin workstation but finds no usable privileged credentials and cannot escalate. The most likely reason:",o:["Luck","A PAM vaulted and rotated the credentials and enforced just-in-time, monitored elevation","The workstation had no network","The attacker was unskilled"],a:1,x:"Vaulting keeps credentials out of the admin's (and thus the attacker's) hands; just-in-time elevation and monitoring block standing privilege abuse."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A contractor account, never disabled after the engagement ended, is used months later from a foreign IP to breach the network. The failed control:",o:["Password complexity","Timely, automated leaver deprovisioning tied to the contract end date","MFA on the firewall","Disk encryption"],a:1,x:"Orphaned accounts are standing backdoors; an automated leaver process linked to the authoritative source would have revoked it immediately."},
{q:"SCENARIO: A leaked repository API key with broad standing access is abused within minutes. The two failures were:",o:["Weak password and no CAPTCHA","Secret hardcoded instead of vaulted, and the key over-privileged instead of least-privilege/short-lived","Slow network and old hardware","No firewall and no antivirus"],a:1,x:"Secrets belong in a vault (or eliminated via short-lived credentials), and machine identities must be least-privilege — both failed here."},
{q:"SCENARIO: Valid credentials access finance data from an unmanaged device in an unusual country at 3 a.m., and the request is denied with step-up required. This demonstrates:",o:["Perimeter trust","Zero Trust continuous, context-aware authorization overriding valid-but-anomalous access","A network outage","Password expiry"],a:1,x:"Conditional access evaluated device, location, time, and risk — denying valid credentials in an anomalous context, the essence of Zero Trust."},
{q:"SCENARIO: An IAM architecture is reviewed by tracing a phished-credential attack through it and finding no single control failure yields catastrophe. This design principle is:",o:["Feature maximization","Defense in depth applied to identity — independent controls (MFA, conditional access, RBAC, PAM, deprovisioning) contain each other's failures","Security through obscurity","Perimeter defense"],a:1,x:"A sound IAM program is judged by containment: the failure of any one identity control is caught by the others, mirroring layered defense."}
]};
